note: "Two rounds. Round 1 over ffb3d2a..9a32ccf raised 11 candidates across five dimensions; adversarial verification refuted 10 and confirmed 1 (reuse, nit): list and page each spelled out the same newest-first-then-filter chain, so the listing rule had two independent definitions. Fixed in f707ee8 - Ring::newest_first owns the ordering, Ring::matching owns id resolution plus the filter, both reads compose them - and fixed rather than waived, since the finding was the author's own to answer. Round 2 re-ran the two dimensions the fix could touch, both empty: reuse confirmed the rule now has exactly one definition and that the extraction is better than the duplication rather than a lateral move (both helpers have two real callers; matching's iterator-taking signature is load-bearing because page must seat the cursor on the unfiltered order); correctness proved the refactor behaviour-preserving by differential harness - the pre-refactor list/page verbatim beside the post-refactor ones over 60,000 randomized ring shapes x 7 filter shapes x every limit 0..n+2 x every cursor (None, each real id, an absent id), comparing page contents, order and next_cursor, with zero divergence. Notable refutations, all on substance rather than taste: an unbounded limit is correct 'at most n' paging because the unparameterised endpoint is unbounded by documented design and the overflow case is already a 400, not a wrap; the T2 doc was accused of leaking Store::page's mechanism and verification found the opposite, every accused phrase being a client-observable consequence while the single lock, the reversed iterator and the peek never left the code; deleting the handler unit tests because the router tests overlap is the substitution docs/testing-strategy.html forbids ('a WU test never excuses unit tests on its commits - coverage is additive'), and the two suites sit at different seams. The load-bearing invariant was independently stress-tested far past the shipped proptest: ~80,000 adversarial interleavings of inserts and byte-cap evictions at every step of the walk, plus 1920 deterministic cases in round 1 that produced 257 genuinely truncated walks with zero skips - which shows the proptest's no-skip branch is not passing vacuously. Efficiency reported nothing by measurement rather than inspection: a paged walk of 200k rows at limit=50 ran 186ms against 95ms for one unpaged list (under 2x, not quadratic), and page cuts the worst-case lock hold from ~95ms to ~105us versus the list it replaces because the clone is bounded by limit. Hostile-input sweep over the route (usize::MAX, overflow, '+5', '0x5', '5.0', Arabic-Indic digits, empty, '-0', whitespace, repeated params, NUL and emoji filters, a zero-uuid cursor, both parameters invalid) produced only named 400s or axum's pre-existing rejection - no panic, no corrupted state. Gate 0 green at both tips. cargo xtask design-scope reports no design artifact, no new surface and no rendered file in the diff, so this note carries no design section. Process notes: the efficiency finder wrote a scratch bench into the working tree despite its read-only instruction (two other finders flagged it rather than reporting it as a finding); it was untracked, was removed, and the diff binding was never affected. Round 2 ran against f707ee8 while the docs-only 4b81bc3 landed; this note is bound to the final tip and 4b81bc3 touches no source file in scope."
